  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nGusty winds are being observed across Southern California, with the \nstrongest winds being observed on mountain foothills and below \npasses and canyons. Top 3 wind gusts over the past 48 hours were \nBuckman Springs with a gust of 82 mph, a 80 mph gust in Hellhole \nCanyon, and a 78 mph gust in Sill Hill. Over the past 12 hours the \ntop 3 wind gusts are Hellhole Canyon, Sill Hill, and Banning with a \ngust of 73 mph. A more comprehensive peak wind gust report is \nheadlined on our weather.gov/sandiego homepage. Winds have peaked \nacross the area and are expected to begin to weaken through this \nevening into Saturday. That being said, winds will remain elevated \ninto Saturday especially for places prone to east winds. Peak wind \ngusts for Saturday will be 35 to 55 mph, with gusts to 60 mph \nconfined to wind prone locations. \n\nElevated to near-critical fire weather conditions will continue \nthrough Saturday, though relative humidity values will increase on \nSaturday. Some influx of moisture from the south will keep relative \nhumidity values above 15% through the remainder of the event. In \nfact, there is a slight chance of light showers for southern San \nDiego County Saturday morning, though there is an even lower chance \n(5-10%) for any wetting rainfall.\n\nHigh temperatures will peak today, gradually decreasing through the \nweekend into early next week. By Tuesday, most locations are \nexpected to be running a few degrees below average near the coast to \nup to 10 degrees below average for inland locations. With those \ncooling conditions we're monitoring for the potential for elevated \nwesterly winds and precipitation. The most likely time for \nprecipitation looks to be Wednesday and Thursday, but some locations \nfurther north have the potential to see precipitation as early as \nTuesday afternoon or evening. Precipitation will likely remain light \nto moderate due to lack of any notable moisture source, though model \nguidance has trended wetter since yesterday. There is still some \ndisagreement on precipitation amounts. We'll have more details in \nthe coming days.",
